a: Entities (4478); Relationships (10781)]. The stoma is explicitly described as an opening that can be used for stool after colostomy and also for urine drainage in urinary diversion procedures [Data: Entities (2797); Relationships (10781)].\\n\\nA combined approach is represented by the double-barreled wet colostomy (DBWC), which uses a single abdominal stoma while keeping urine and stool separate until they exit [Data: Entities (4898, 2797); Relationships (10798)]. This positions “stoma” as a bridging node between fecal and urinary diversion sub-networks [Data: Entities (2797, 2796); Relationships (5171, 10781)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Continent cutaneous pouch (Indiana pouch) management depends on catheterization via a stoma and a one-way valve\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The continent cutaneous pouch (Indiana pouch) is described as a urinary diversion creating an internal urine-holding pouch connected to a stoma via an intestinal channel, drained several times daily by inserting a catheter through the stoma and a one-way valve (without an external ostomy bag) [Data: Entities (5911, 5926, 5908); Relationships (13455, 13447)].\\n\\nSupporting relationships connect the Indiana pouch to Indiana University as its development origin, and include ancillary care details such as covering the stoma with a small bandage in this setup [Data: Entities (5903, 5909, 2797); Relationships (13430, 13433)]. Collectively, these nodes reflect a “technical capability” cluster describing the functional components (stoma access + valve mechanism + intermittent catheterization) required to operate a continent diversion system [Data: Entities (5911, 5926, 5908); Relationships (13435, 13447, 13455)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Procedure-related complications cluster around biopsy: bleeding (rectum/urine/stool/semen) and infection, typically temporary\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The complications node describes unwanted outcomes during/after procedures such a